HTTP: served by the core, routed to whichever module asked
==========================================================

Phase 5 of doc/proposals/006-comunicaciones-unificadas.md.  The server is
Mongoose, on a worker thread of its own; modules claim routes on it and
never see a socket.

The core serves it rather than a module.  HTTP is not a thing a server
either has or has not: a listener is one of the server's own ports, it has
to come up before any module is loaded and stay up across a rehash that
unloads one, and its TLS is configuration and not code.  What is
pluggable is the routes, and those are what modules bring.


WHY A LIBRARY
=============

This replaced a hand-written parser and poll() loop that worked.  The
argument is not that it was broken; it is that HTTP is a protocol where
being nearly right is a security bug, and the list of ways to get framing
wrong -- chunked encoding, folded headers, pipelining, Content-Length
against Transfer-Encoding -- is a list somebody else has already walked,
with fuzzers, for twenty years.

What came with it, none of it written here: chunked request bodies, TLS,
and WebSockets and SSE for when they are wanted.

The rule that follows: ircd/mongoose/ is upstream's amalgamation and is
never edited.  A local patch would give away the only thing this is for.
Updating is replacing the two files and running the tests.  Mongoose is
GPL-2.0-only, so an ircd built with it is GPL-2.0 rather than "2 or
later"; see ircd/mongoose/README.


THE TWO SIDES
=============

The **server** is ircd/http_server.c: the only file in the tree that knows
Mongoose exists.  It owns the socket, the parsing and the TLS, on a
thread of its own, and hands the main thread requests that are already
parsed.

The **consumer** claims routes:

  http_add_route(mod, "POST", "/upload", handle_upload, self)

and checks, from HOOK_CONFIG_LOADED:

  if (!http_available())
    /* say so in the log; switch that part off; carry on */

It does not fail to load and it does not wait.  The part of it that needed
HTTP is off and the rest works.  That is the contract db.h has too, and it
is what keeps "nobody set HTTP_PORT" from being an outage.

http_available() answers from HTTP_PORT, not from the socket: a consumer
is asking whether its route will ever be reached, and a listener that is
down for the length of a rehash is not a module's business.  Ask it from
HOOK_CONFIG_LOADED rather than mi_init, which runs in the middle of the
parse where the features are not final -- that is the one mistake here
that is silent.

A route may be claimed while nothing is listening.  The module that wants
one loads first as often as not, and making that an error would make load
order part of the configuration.


ROUTING
=======

A path ending in `/` matches everything under it; anything else matches
exactly.  An exact match beats a prefix, and a longer prefix beats a
shorter one:

  /files/index        matches only that
  /files/thumb/       matches /files/thumb/anything
  /files/             matches everything else under /files/

That is the whole routing language, on purpose.  A pattern syntax is a
thing every consumer would have to learn and every server would have to
agree about, and nothing here has needed one.

The method is part of the route.  Two claims on the same method and path
are refused: two handlers for one route is a question with no right
answer.

Nothing claimed means the core never owned the request: http_dispatch()
says so, the server sends its own 404, and there is nothing to clean up.


ANSWERING LATER
===============

A handler runs in the main thread and does **not** have to answer there.
Authorising an upload means asking a database, and a handler that blocked
for it would stop every client on the server.  So it is given an
http_req_t and it either

  - fills in the response and returns non-zero, or
  - keeps the identifier, returns zero, and calls http_respond() when the
    answer arrives.

Every request has a deadline -- HTTP_TIMEOUT, five seconds by default --
on a timer of the core's own.  One that passes is answered 504 and a later
answer is dropped.  A client holding a socket open because a module forgot
about it is the failure the deadline exists to bound; answering late is
not a crash, because a module that answers late has been slow rather than
wrong.


WHAT UNLOADING TAKES
====================

A consumer unloading takes its routes, and the requests it will never
answer are handed back to the transport -- ht_cancel() -- so the
connection is let go rather than held to the deadline.

The listener going down -- HTTP_PORT set to 0, or the server shutting
down -- takes every request in flight with it, silently: there is nobody
left to answer through.  The routes stay, and wait for it to come back.


LIMITS
======

  HTTP_BODY_MAX       65536   what crosses into the main thread
  HTTP_HEADERS_MAX    32      headers in either direction
  HTTP_PATH_MAX       255
  HTTP_ROUTES_MAX     64      per module

HTTP_BODY_MAX is not a limit on what HTTP can transfer.  It is a limit on
what the event loop holds: a file upload is streamed to storage by the
server and its handler is given the metadata, because a hundred
megabytes buffered here would be a hundred megabytes every other client
waits behind.


CONFIGURING IT
==============

  F:HTTP_PORT:8080
  F:HTTP_BIND:127.0.0.1
  F:HTTP_MAX_CLIENTS:64
  F:HTTP_TLS_CERT:/etc/ircd/http-cert.pem
  F:HTTP_TLS_KEY:/etc/ircd/http-key.pem

and WORKER_THREADS at one or more, because the listener is a thread.

  HTTP_PORT           0       where to listen; 0 is off
  HTTP_BIND           ""      what to bind to; empty is every address
  HTTP_MAX_CLIENTS    64      connections at once, past which new ones
                              are closed rather than queued
  HTTP_TLS_CERT       ""      PEM certificate; both or neither
  HTTP_TLS_KEY        ""      PEM private key

HTTP_PORT is 0 by default and at 0 nothing listens.  A server does not
open a second port because a feature exists; an operator says where.

The listener follows the configuration.  It is reconciled once the file
has been read in full -- at start-up and after every rehash, never in the
middle of a parse -- and a rehash that changed none of the five settings
above does nothing at all, because restarting a listener drops every
connection on it.

TLS is Mongoose's, not the ircd's.  IRCU_TLS picks what the *IRC* ports
speak and can be gnutls, or none, so the HTTP side reads its own PEM
files.  Set both or neither; one alone is half a configuration and is
read as "no TLS".

Which TLS it is depends on the build, and so does what certificate it
will take:

  IRCU_TLS=openssl    Mongoose uses OpenSSL, which the ircd already links
                      -- one TLS library in the process, not two.  RSA or
                      ECDSA, whatever OpenSSL takes.
  anything else       Mongoose's own: self-contained, TLS 1.3 only, and
                      **ECDSA only**.  An RSA certificate is refused at
                      the handshake, which from the client looks like the
                      connection being dropped.

    openssl req -x509 -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:prime256v1 \
      -keyout http-key.pem -out http-cert.pem -days 365 -nodes \
      -subj "/CN=irc.example.net"

That asymmetry is worth knowing before it is diagnosed at three in the
morning: the same PEM files that work on an openssl build fail on a
gnutls one.


WHICH THREAD IS WHICH
=====================

  the socket           the worker's, always
  the routes           the main thread's, always
  a request going up   worker_post(), which runs the dispatch in the main
                       thread
  an answer coming     mg_wakeup(), which Mongoose documents as safe from
  back                 any thread and which wakes its poll

There is no queue and no self-pipe here: the library already has one.

Across that line travels a struct of bytes and a connection id -- an
integer Mongoose guarantees unique -- never a pointer.  A connection that
went away while its answer was being worked out simply is not found, which
is the ordinary case for a client that pressed stop.

Nothing on the worker touches core state: no struct Client, no
CurrentTime, no MyMalloc(), no log_write(), no sendto_*.  Mongoose's own
logging is compiled out for the same reason, and what it has to say about
a failed listen comes back as the task it posts.


WHAT IT WILL NOT READ
=====================

Mongoose does the framing.  What it deliberately does not do is decide
what a path *means*, and that decision is ircd/http_server.c's:

  ".." in a path        400.  Nothing here opens a file, but a path is
                        handed to modules and one of them will, so the
                        check belongs where the path is first believed.
  "%2F", "%5C"          400.  Routing happens on the decoded path, so an
                        encoded separator would turn what the client
                        wrote as data into a separator and reach a prefix
                        route it is not under.
  "%00"                 400.  It shortens the path for whoever reads it
                        with str*() next.
  a control byte        400.
  a body over 64 KiB    413 -- HTTP_BODY_MAX, below.

The path a handler is given is decoded; the query string is not, because
"+" means a space there and nowhere else and a server that decided would
be deciding for every route at once.

A response header whose name or value holds a newline is dropped and the
response still sent: a newline there is how one response becomes two, and
the second one is whatever the module was handed.

http_t covers the routes, the deadline and what unloading takes, with no
socket at all -- the split ircd/migration.c has from migration_run.c.
There is no unit test of the parser any more, because the parser is not
ours; that is the trade.


WHAT THIS IS FOR
================

File upload and download with object storage behind it, with the ircd
issuing a short-lived token -- HMAC-SHA256 or AES-256-GCM from
include/ircd_sha256.h and include/ircd_aes.h -- bound to the account and
the channel, which the route validates.  Incoming webhooks and a REST
API for integrations are routes on the same listener.

Files are deferred for now.  What is here is the server and the routes on
it -- the part that has to exist before any of that can be written as a
module of its own, and the part that webhooks and a REST API need and
nothing more.
